\section{What it tests}{

For each tested branch, whether some sites evolved with omega above 1 on that branch.
aBSREL fits a different number of omega classes per branch, chosen by AICc. A
significant branch is evidence under the model for episodic positive selection on some
sites of that branch, not across the gene, and not proof of adaptation.
}

\section{Branches tested}{

\code{branches = "all"} (default) tests every branch: the exploratory analysis for comparing
lineages, which spends power on many tests. \code{"foreground"} tests only the foreground
hypothesis, as in \code{\link[=phylo_busted]{phylo_busted()}}, which is more powerful when the hypothesis was fixed
in advance. Branch identity follows the species tree: a branch of the pruned tree is
named by the species-tree branches it joins (\code{species_tree_branches}).
}

\section{Multiple testing}{

Three levels, all reported. \code{p_raw} is HyPhy's uncorrected p-value for a branch.
\code{p_holm} is HyPhy's correction across the branches tested in that orthogroup: the k-th
smallest of m p-values times (m - k + 1), without Holm's running maximum, so it lies
between Holm's and Hochberg's procedures. Per orthogroup, \code{p_value} is the smallest
\code{p_holm}, in effect Hochberg's test that some tested branch was under selection (valid
when the branches' p-values are independent or positively dependent), and \code{p_adj}
applies Benjamini-Hochberg to it across
orthogroups. Per lineage, \code{p_bh_lineage} applies Benjamini-Hochberg to \code{p_raw} across the
orthogroups in which that species-tree branch was tested: use it to ask in how many genes
a given lineage shows episodic selection.
}

\section{Outputs}{

In \code{file.path(output_dir, run_name)}: \verb{_results.tsv} (one row per orthogroup),
\verb{_branches.tsv} (one row per orthogroup and branch, with the baseline omega of every
branch and the test results of tested branches), \verb{_sequences.tsv}, \verb{_provenance.json},
and \verb{hyphy/<orthogroup>/} with HyPhy's inputs, log, and JSON.
}

